    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d701">

                <pb facs="0956" n="842" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg842"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.415 Traders/30</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of the British Embassy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Osborne</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Castle</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-11-17">17 November, 1931</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Assistant Secretary</hi>: You may remember
                    that I wrote you a Private and Confidential letter on August 6th asking if you
                    could let us have at least a provisional expression of your opinion on the two
                    alternative draft treaties regarding the admission of Australian business men to
                    the United States, which were handed to you by Sir Ronald Lindsay on April 27th
                    last.</p>
                <pb facs="0957" n="843" xml:id="pg843"/>
                <p>The Foreign Office are pressing us for a reply and I should be very grateful to
                    you for anything you could tell me for communication to London.</p>
                <p>Believe me [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">D. G. Osborne</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
